<!--
  Litestone page § One schema. Facts: packages/litestone/README.md (Why),
  docs/typescript.md (what gets generated), docs/jsonschema.md, docs/validate.md
  (the client-boundary validators leave no CHECK behind), docs/cli.md (migrate
  create, migrate check, types, jsonschema). The proof is a real run of the CLI
  over a two-model schema, then one line added to it: the migration, the types
  and the JSON Schema each changed by that line, and the client refused a bad
  write. Output is abridged by hand to the lines the edit moved.
  Not claimed: a CHECK for @length or @email (they are enforced by the client and
  written into the JSON Schema, not into the table), and a type for
  Create that makes an array optional (tags has a SQL default and is still
  required on the type).
  The samples sit in consts: a brace in markup opens a Mesa expression.
-->
<script>
  const SCHEMA = `// db/schema.lite, abridged
model Author {
  email    String   @unique @email
  // ...
}

model Post {
  id       Int      @id
  title    String   @length(3, 120)
  status   Status   @default(draft)
  summary  String?  @length(0, 280)   // the one line added
  authorId Int
  // ...
}`

  const OUTPUT = `$ litestone migrate create summary
ALTER TABLE "post" ADD COLUMN "summary" TEXT;

$ litestone types
export interface Post {
  summary?: string | null
}
export interface PostCreate {
  status?: Status          // has a default
  summary?: string | null
}

$ litestone jsonschema
"summary": { "anyOf": [
  { "type": "string", "minLength": 0, "maxLength": 280 },
  { "type": "null" } ] }

db.author.create({ data: { email: 'not-an-email', ... } })
Validation failed — email: must be a valid email address`
</script>

<section id="one-file" class="bc-section">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">One schema</p>
    <h2>Change a line once. Nothing else to keep in step.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Your data model usually lives in an ORM schema, a validator, TypeScript types, a JSON Schema
      and migration files, and they drift. In Litestone it lives in one <code>.lite</code> file,
      and the other four are written from it.
    </p>

    <div class="bc-split">
      <ul class="bc-points">
        <li>
          <strong>One edit, every output.</strong>
          Add a column and its migration, its TypeScript types and its JSON Schema all pick it
          up the next time you run them. The client reads the same file when your app starts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A rule is written once.</strong>
          <code>@email</code> and <code>@length(3, 120)</code> refuse a bad write in the client
          and become the matching JSON Schema keywords, so a form or an API checks the same
          rule your database layer does.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Types you never maintain.</strong>
          Each model gets a row type and create, update, where and order-by types, and a field
          with a default is optional on create.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A stale migration fails in CI.</strong>
          <code>litestone migrate check</code> exits 1 when the migration files do not build the
          schema you declared, with no database needed, so the gap is found before you deploy.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="bc-proof">
        <pre class="code">{SCHEMA}</pre>
        <pre class="code">{OUTPUT}</pre>
        <figcaption>
          A real run of the <code>litestone</code> CLI over a two-model schema, then the one
          line marked above added to it. Shown abridged: only the lines that edit moved.
          <code>@length</code> and <code>@email</code> are enforced by the client and written
          into the JSON Schema, not into the table.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="bc-more">
      Read more:
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/typescript.md">generated types</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/jsonschema.md">JSON Schema output</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/migrations.md">migrations</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/validate.md">validators</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/cli.md">the CLI</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .bc-proof pre.code { overflow-x: auto; font-size: .82rem; margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
</style>
